title The elder record did not have to move, so the port is a lift
prompt the standing baton -- open the card, read the rota row, take the lane's crux
rota lap 4268, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; the happy zone and the thin edge taught this lap its shape. A contract check at a seam, proven with no disk, no CLI and no format change, is exactly the zone doing what the teaching says it can -- and it is why the answer to a gated question could be produced without touching the gate.
think Pheromone asked this seat by name -- main.rye inlines a model weave.rye owns, port or drop -- and REDS %589 holds the disagreement under a ceiling because reconciling was read as rewriting the shipped .mantra/ record.
think Reading both files rather than the ledger row: main.rye is a single-site CLI whose apply reads every position off one counter, so site and run are not ABSENT from the elder record, they are CONSTANT across it. That is a different fact and it changes the answer.
think Place.less_than reads run, then site, then pos. Hold the first two constant and it reduces to pos <, which is exactly main.rye's own current() sort. So the lifted document IS the elder document, and the port is a widening rather than a conversion.
think Wrote Weave.from_v1 as that sentence in code, plus V1Row, v1_site and v1_run, with two new named errors. Nothing existing moved; main.rye and the on-disk format are untouched.
think The lift adds a refusal the elder reader has never had -- deserialize_weave appends rows in blob order and asks nothing, so a hand-edited blob reaches current()'s ordering assert as a crash rather than an error.
think Adding a pub fn made the head guard answer, and it answered wrong: declared=5 listed=5 verdict=ok over a module publishing six. The name class [a-z_]+ fed BOTH readings, so a name carrying a digit was invisible to the mirror built to catch exactly this.
obs Symmetric blindness reads healthy. head_missing and head_stale were built as mirrors so a fault in one direction could not hide in the other; one shared character class defeated both at once. That is the whole of REDS %612, and the elder scan replayed in a pen still answers 5/5 ok on this module.
obs A ceiling is not always a gate on the work it names. %589's ceiling was read as saying the port is closed; what it actually says is that the two copies disagree. Whether closing that gap costs a format break was never measured, and measuring it took one function and one witness.
obs Named against myself, and it BIT: I wrote the cold roster to /tmp/cold_open.txt, a constant name on a pier eight ships share -- REDS %549's exact fault, minutes after reading that row on the card. The file came back holding a duplicated twenty-line block AND a closing run_verdict, and I put that verdict's guard counts on the live card. The run lock disproved them: pid 3514949, cwd this tree, started 05:12:45, still alive -- my own cold pass, nowhere near its close, so the verdict I had read was somebody else's.
obs And the sting is in what happened next. My own pass closed at 159 guards, 1,263s, 155 green, 2 red, 2 gated -- the borrowed counts EXACTLY. So the card was never wrong, and nothing would ever have told me. A borrowed reading that happens to agree is worse than one that disagrees: the disagreeing one is caught by the next reader, and the agreeing one becomes evidence. %549 says a lap writes its own output under its own root; the reason is not that a shared file is usually wrong, it is that a shared file is never checkable.
obs The cold pass ran while this lap edited the tree, against the card's hold-still directive. The hot close is what measures the tree the commit ships, so the reading that matters is honest; the discipline slip is named rather than covered.
loom witness=mantra_weave_v1_lift claims=10 refusals=4 control_phases=15 control_wall_s=16
